{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BJen9Fcv3hs8HRrK1uM9vt","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis extends `ard setup` in two ways. It sets the repository variable for the bot login, taken from the App identity, through a new effect-layer call. It also checks the repository's bot-commits workflow against a copy embedded in ard and adds a flag that prints that copy. Both dependencies (the first `ard setup` command in `internal/setupcmd` and the GitHub connectivity and token work) are already in this checkout, so the work can start now. The engineer triage role is the only planner, so it plans the whole change.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: implementation steps for the new effect-layer method, the variable check and apply (including asking before replacing a different existing value), the embedded workflow comparison and the print flag, plus tests and doc updates.\n- worker engineer: Go change in the setup command and effect layer, with unit tests.\n\n## Open issues\n- The change only reads the existing workflow file under `.github/workflows/` and embeds a copy of it in ard. It must not edit anything under `.github/`. If the embedded copy needs to differ from the committed file, a human commits the change to the workflow.\n- The CLI gains a new flag and setup gains a new apply step. No product-manager, designer or documentation-planner role exists, so the engineer plan has to cover the user-facing messages and the doc updates.\ninternal/effects:\napprove.go\napprove_test.go\narchtest\ndryrun.go\neffects.go\neffects_test.go\nledger.go\nlive.go\nopen.go\n\ninternal/setupcmd:\nappmanifest.go\ncallback.go\ncmd.go\ncmd_test.go\nconvert.go\ncreateapp.go\ncreateapp_test.go\nkeyfile.go\npolicy.go\npolicy_test.go\nreport.go\nsetup.go\nsetup_test.go\nbot-commits.yml\nci.yml\ndocs.yml\nrelease.yml\nversion.yml\n      93 internal/setupcmd/appmanifest.go\n      91 internal/setupcmd/callback.go\n     116 internal/setupcmd/cmd.go\n     103 internal/setupcmd/cmd_test.go\n      73 internal/setupcmd/convert.go\n     224 internal/setupcmd/createapp.go\n     394 internal/setupcmd/createapp_test.go\n      86 internal/setupcmd/keyfile.go\n     112 internal/setupcmd/policy.go\n     221 internal/setupcmd/policy_test.go\n      67 internal/setupcmd/report.go\n     180 internal/setupcmd/setup.go\n     186 internal/setupcmd/setup_test.go\n    1946 total\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*\n22:  protected_paths:\n23-    - .github/\n24-    - .ard/config.yaml\n25-    - .ard/scanners/\n26-    - .ard/triage/\n27-    - .ard/workers/\n28-    - .ard/reviewers/\n29-    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n30-    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code (and humans) working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nard is a controller that automates repository development: scanners → triage → worker → reviewers → merger, coordinated through `ard:` GitHub labels. The design is [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md). Read the relevant section before changing behaviour.\n\n**docs/proposal.md is the source of truth for design.** A PR that changes behaviour updates the proposal in the same PR. If code and proposal disagree, raise it on the issue rather than silently picking one.\n\n## Invariants — never violate\n\n1. **The controller owns every side effect.** Only controller code calls GitHub or runs `git commit`/`push`/`merge`. Agents edit files in a worktree and report through `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` frontmatter; they never touch labels, comments or branches.\n2. **Agents hold no GitHub token.** Credentials stay in the controller and executor.\n3. **The main-branch committer writes only `.ard/<issue#>/`.** Config (`.ard/config.yaml`) and role directories (`.ard/<type>/<name>/`) are human-owned. Only `ard init` may write them: into the local working tree for a human to commit, or (`--pr`) on the `ard-init` branch through `committer.Init` and a PR a human merges; ard never writes main. Workers' diff guard, the committer's path guards and the merger's protected paths enforce it.\n4. **Every label is prefixed `ard:`.** Stage and role labels follow `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]`. The only labels outside that pattern are the flat labels the proposal lists (such as `ard:pending`, `ard:hold` and `ard:approved`); add a new one to that list in the same PR (see the label table in the proposal).\n5. **Every side effect honours dry-run.** Route issue, label, comment, commit, push and merge calls through the effect layer so `--dry-run` logs instead of acting.\n6. **Untrusted text is data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text reach agents as files marked untrusted, never spliced into prompts.\n7. **Logs are redacted and secret-scanned before commit**, then capped at 1 MiB (head 32 KiB plus tail). The scan fails closed.\n\n## Stack\n\n- Go, a single static `ard` binary (CGO off; use a pure-Go SQLite driver such as `modernc.org/sqlite`).\n- Supported platforms: macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported (the local executor relies on `flock` and POSIX process handling).\n- GitHub access through a GitHub App installation token, behind an interface so tests use a fake.\n- The language choice is tracked as a decision issue; if it changes, update this file.\n\n## Layout\n\n[docs/layout.md](docs/layout.md) lists every directory and package and what it holds. Read it to find where code lives; a PR that adds, removes or renames a directory or package updates that file, not this one.\n\nNew subcommands: implement the body in its own package as\n`func(ctx context.Context, e *cli.Env) error`, then add one field to\n`cli.Handlers` and one wiring line in `cmd/ard/main.go`'s `handlers()`.\nA nil handler runs the command as a \"not implemented yet\" stub.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nmake build   # bin/ard","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7aad3249